{% callout tag="Info" title="This is the how-to companion to [Your codebase is the prompt](/writing/codebase-context-engineering-your-codebase-is-the-prompt) — where I argue that your codebase, not your prompts, is what makes agents productive. That piece is the *why*; this series is the *how*. You don't have to read it first, but that's where the case lives." %}
{% /callout %}

The premise, in one line: **treat a coding agent like a junior dev you onboard several times an hour.** Same shaky long-term memory, same need for guardrails — except you're now doing the onboarding fifty times a day instead of once a quarter. Which turns the problem into one every team lead already knows: what makes a new hire productive *fast*, while keeping their output consistent enough that you can review it at a glance?

From experience, five things move the needle:

1. a logically **structured codebase** — files in the right place, named the right way, that feels natural to explore;
2. **technology and frameworks** that fit the project, lower cognitive load, and are well documented;
3. concise, complete, up-to-date **documentation**;
4. clear **processes and rules**, with tooling that actually enforces them;
5. a solid set of **examples** — real code that conveys your style better than any rule could.

This series is about building each of these into the codebase itself, so you have to spell out as little as possible. This first part covers the two **silent foundations**: structure and technology. Silent, because you set them once and they shape every agent interaction afterwards — without you writing a single rule. Get them right and a good chunk of your "context engineering" is done for free.

## Structure goes a long way

I'll admit it: I never start by reading the `README.md` when I open an unfamiliar project. I go straight to the code — the folder layout, the naming scheme, the overall organisation. Is the frontend split by feature or by atomic design? Is it a monorepo? Is there shared code, and where does it live?

**Reading a codebase's structure is like walking through a house: you can tell whether the previous owner took care of the place.** A crooked door here, a patch of mould there, and you immediately want to check the roof and the wiring — neglect tends to spread.

The reverse is just as telling. Some codebases simply *feel right*: files and features are exactly where you'd reach for them. That signals the people before you had taste, and my trust in their work — down to the smallest method — climbs accordingly.

A tasteful structure does the same job for your agents. It distills your taste into their output and **enables copy/paste-driven development**: asked for a new dashboard page, the agent finds the existing pages, the existing components, the existing API calls, and mirrors them. It removes choices — almost always a good thing, for agents and teammates alike. Determinism wins.

It also **makes review faster**: a misplaced module, or a file that doesn't fit the pattern, jumps straight out at you.

So **take your time tailoring the structure to the project.** Don't rush it, and don't hand the job to your agent — be active here. If you have a structure that's proven itself on a past project, reuse it. (Reuse is going to be a recurring theme.)

In our boilerplate, the structure is documented for both the [backend](https://lonestone.github.io/lonestone-boilerplate/references/backend/) and the [frontend](https://lonestone.github.io/lonestone-boilerplate/references/frontend/). It began as a way to help developers move between projects; with agents it pays off twice, because a shared structure lets us write skills and rules that apply to *every* project built on the boilerplate, instead of one-off, per-project ones.

