## The right stack does half the work

The choice of language, framework and tooling can lift your agents' quality and speed even more than it lifts your human colleagues'. Three reasons stand out.

### A stricter stack means fewer rules to write

[Strongly typed languages play straight to an LLM's strengths](https://github.blog/ai-and-ml/llms/why-ai-is-pushing-developers-toward-typed-languages/): the type system catches mistakes the moment they're made and tightens the feedback loop. TypeScript is the obvious pick; Rust is another. The principle underneath: **the stricter your language and tooling, the fewer custom rules you have to write and maintain. That's context engineering bundled into the tech.**

Your linter is part of the same bargain. It catches code smells faster and more consistently than you ever will in review — and it does so *deterministically*. A few lines of lint config replace pages of a `RULES.md` you'd otherwise have to write, keep current, and pay for in context on every run. We run TypeScript end-to-end and lean on a strict, batteries-included config (currently [Antfu's](https://github.com/antfu/eslint-config)) so we hand-write almost no style rules. The specific config matters far less than the stance: pick strict defaults and let them do the talking.

{% callout tag="Tip" title="Your style rules still matter" %}
You **will** read the code your agent produces — in review, or months later chasing a bug. Type and lint rules aren't bureaucracy; they're what keep that code legible when you need it most.
{% /callout %}

### A fast feedback loop keeps everyone in flow

Speed has always mattered for the tools you run constantly — builders, linters, test runners. With agents it matters more, for two reasons.

First, when you're working alongside an agent, a slow check is the difference between **staying in flow and opening a new browser tab** while you wait — all the more so because an agent can hand you a large chunk of code at once.

Second, **agents run these tools far more often than you would.** Where your team might run the suite ten times a day, agents trigger it many times more, across many more pull requests and CI runs. Small per-run savings compound fast.

{% figure src="/writing/codebase-context-engineering-pt-1-the-silent-foundations/cleanshot-2026-03-16-at-16-00-33.png" alt="Tweet by @thekitze: \"who gives a fuck about code formatting in 2026 lol\", quoting daily.dev: \"oxfmt is 30x faster than prettier. most devs will stick with prettier because we're tired of switching tools every 3 months for nothing\"." kind="capture" /%}
> Contrary [to some](https://x.com/thekitze/status/2033315753470857220), I am convinced that picking the right tools is more important than ever.

So treat speed as a feature when you pick your tools, not a bonus. Reaching for faster options — an [oxlint](https://oxc.rs/docs/guide/usage/linter.html)-class linter, [Vitest](https://vitest.dev) over a slower runner — isn't about chasing the bleeding edge; it directly protects your flow and your CI bill. (It's why we moved our NestJS backend onto SWC: faster startup, and Vitest instead of Jest.)

### Popular, well-documented tools are easier for agents

A framework with a large following is a real advantage — the model has simply seen far more of it. Reaching for the mainstream option over a niche one isn't the boldest choice, and it can cost you a little innovation, but it's a fair trade when you're optimising for predictable output.

Popularity isn't the whole story, though. **A tool's documentation, its API, and its general ease of use matter just as much.** A concept that's hard for a human to hold in their head — React's `useEffect` is the classic example — tends to trip up an agent just the same. Favour tools with a simple mental model and good docs.

{% callout tag="Info" title="What about doc-fetching MCPs?" %}
I rarely wire up an MCP just to feed documentation. More and more libraries ship a "copy as Markdown" button, and I'm happy to let agents browse the docs themselves — a few more tokens, but one less moving part to maintain.
{% /callout %}

## Wiring it together is the hard part

Picking good tools isn't enough — you have to make them cooperate, and that's where the real work usually hides. Sometimes it's an ESM-only dependency that has to live inside a CommonJS codebase (hello, NestJS). Sometimes it's subtler: getting a "magic" auth library to coexist with the level of control we wanted from our ORM took several iterations and more than one team discussion. The frontend is no kinder — React, forms, state, data fetching: making that many libraries play together is genuine work.

So, once more: **don't rush.** Find the combination that fits your team and your projects — not just technically, but until the whole thing *clicks*. That upfront investment pays itself back almost immediately, and every agent you point at the result inherits it for free.

---

Structure and stack are the *silent* foundations: they carry most of the weight without you writing a single rule. But silent only goes so far. At some point you have to put the rest of your decisions into words — and make sure they're actually followed. That's the next piece.
